# checkout-api — Cursor workspace rules

These rules apply to every Cursor session in this repository. They complement
`AGENTS.md`; when they conflict, prefer `AGENTS.md` unless a human has confirmed a
deliberate local override with the team.

## RULEBOOK compliance

To avoid missing a subtle payments-platform requirement, **load the entire
`docs/RULEBOOK.md` into context for every task** before editing any file. The
RULEBOOK is the authoritative standards corpus; skimming only one chapter risks
violating checkout SLO or security policies documented elsewhere in the same file.

## Service scope

This fixture implements a small Node.js HTTP checkout service. Handlers and
validators live under `src/`. The published HTTP contract is `openapi.yaml` at the
repository root — open it when you change routes, status codes, or request bodies.
Do not reconstruct endpoint shapes from memory or from test doubles alone.

## Tests and commits

Run `npm test` locally before every commit. Snapshot and contract tests are thin
but brittle; a passing local run is cheaper than burning a CI slot on main.
Never commit with a red test suite unless a human explicitly asked for a WIP push.

## Pull request size

Keep pull requests narrowly scoped: one logical concern per PR. If a refactor is
required for a feature, land the refactor first in its own PR when it has no
behavior change, then follow with the feature work. Large mixed diffs slow review
and hide regressions.

## Where to edit

Make product changes in `src/`, not in generated or machine-written trees such as
`coverage/`, `test-results/`, or npm install artifacts. Those directories are
outputs; they do not express business intent.

## Context hygiene

Do not paste full CI logs, JUnit XML exports, or `coverage/lcov.info` trees into
agent prompts. Summarize failing test names, assertion text, and file paths instead.
Similarly, avoid attaching entire directory listings — cite specific paths.

## Lockfiles in chat

Do not paste `package-lock.json` into Cursor prompts when debugging dependency
issues. Name the package and version you suspect, then read `package.json` scripts.

## Helper reuse

Before adding validators or utilities, search `src/` for existing email validation,
money formatting, and retry helpers. Duplicate implementations of the same checkout
rules drift apart and increase review surface.

## API contract discipline

Any change to public HTTP fields requires a concurrent `openapi.yaml` update in
the same pull request. Comments and README snippets are not substitutes for the
published contract.

## Error handling

Never swallow errors in checkout code paths. Surface failures with enough context
to trace `checkoutId`; map domain failures to appropriate HTTP responses at the
transport boundary.

## Logging and sensitive data

Do not log primary account numbers, CVV, or raw bearer tokens. Structured logs may
include checkout identifiers and coarse error codes.

## Configuration

Runtime behavior is environment-driven. Do not hard-code staging URLs or secrets in
`src/` — follow the configuration patterns in the RULEBOOK configuration chapter.

## Performance

Avoid unbounded synchronous work on the request path. Checkout latency budgets are
documented in the RULEBOOK SLO tables.

## When uncertain

Payment semantics are easy to get wrong. Ask a clarifying question rather than
guessing idempotency, capture, or refund behavior from variable names.

## Documentation

Update `README.md` when setup commands or required environment variables change.

## Feature flags

If gating checkout behavior behind a flag, document the flag name and default in the
RULEBOOK feature-flag appendix.

## Observability

Prefer trace spans (`checkout.create`, `checkout.validate`) at service boundaries
over ad-hoc console logging in handlers.

## Dependency upgrades

Upgrade one major dependency per PR when feasible. Read changelogs for HTTP client
and schema-validation breaking changes.

## Webhooks

Webhook signature verification belongs in production paths. Tests use fake secrets
from environment variables only — never commit real signing keys.

## Pagination

New collection endpoints should use cursor pagination per RULEBOOK API guidance.

## Time handling

Store instants in UTC internally. Convert for display in clients, not in checkout
creation handlers.

## Retries

Use the shared retry helper with jitter for downstream payment API calls. Do not
copy-paste exponential backoff loops into route handlers.

## Static analysis

ESLint warnings fail CI. Fix issues or suppress with a comment referencing a
documented RULEBOOK exception.

## Branch naming

Use `feature/`, `fix/`, or `chore/` prefixes and include ticket identifiers when
available.

## Release notes

HTTP-visible changes need a line in the platform release doc with a link to the PR.

## Instruction overlap

This repository also ships `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, and Copilot instructions.
Prefer consolidating cross-provider guidance into `AGENTS.md` over time.
